  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 AM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nA weak cold front/trough axis is sliding across the Northland \nearly this morning, which will be followed by a large high \npressure ridge building into the area today and tonight. This \nhigh pressure will bring a couple days of pleasant early fall \nweather with cooler than normal temperatures. Highs today will \nonly be in the upper 50s to mid 60s across the region. As the \nhigh pressure settles in tonight, skies will clear and winds \nwill become light, setting the stage for good radiational \ncooling. Cirrus moving in from the west ahead of our next \nprecipitation system could dampen the cooling effects as we get \ntowards morning. Otherwise, temperatures should drop into the \n30s across many inland areas, resulting in patchy frost \ndevelopment over parts of northern Minnesota and northwest \nWisconsin by early Monday morning. \n\nA strong upper low pressure system moves from the northern \nPlains across the southern Canadian Prairies and into northern \nOntario Monday through Tuesday. This system will bring a surge \nof moisture into the region and spread a round of widespread \nshowers and embedded thunderstorms across our area Monday \nafternoon and continue Monday night. Rainfall amounts of one \ninch or greater are likely (60-80% chance) for areas south of \nthe Iron Range and northwest Wisconsin. There is a marginal risk\nfor elevated hail producing storms Monday evening for parts of \nMonday evening with a strong low level jet and some elevated \ninstability, but at least some of this depends on how far north \nthe surface boundary to our south can get, and how far north the\nelevated instability can be advected. We will also have a \nmarginal risk of excessive rainfall. There will be PWAT values \nof 1-2\", which would be in the 90th percentile or greater of \nclimatology for the upper air site in International Falls. At \nthis time, the ECMWF EFI is only showing QPF values in the \n60-70th percentile, which decreases confidence in very heavy \nrainfall. The current QPF forecast maintains the NBM solution, \nwhich brings a broad 0.4 to 1.25\" across the CWA on Monday \nthrough Monday night. Our forecast heaviest rainfall amounts are\nnot co-located with the rainfall we got Friday night, reducing \nconcerns for flooding. \n\nThe rainfall will come to an end by Tuesday, with a cold front \nbringing gusty westerly winds and another day of near to below \nnormal high temperatures. Expect clearing skies as drier air \nfilters into the area, leading to a mostly sunny Tuesday \nafternoon.\n\nA relatively quiet and seasonable pattern takes hold for the \nremainder of the work week, with high pressure building in again\nfor Wednesday. Chances for rain showers return Wednesday night \ninto Thursday, and again on Friday and Friday night, as weak \nsystems slide through the mostly zonal flow aloft. High \ntemperatures Wednesday will be in the 60s for most locations, \nbut should gradually moderate through the end of the week so \nhighs on Friday and Saturday should be in the mid 60s to low \n70s.",
